---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Perception Lab | tcplot: Data Visualization in Minneapolis and St. Paul"
    description="Replicate classic visual-perception experiments, run them on the room, and chart our own error rates — the morning the attendees are the dataset."
>
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-8 mb-12">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">
                Upcoming: Perception Lab
            </h2>

            <div class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
                <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">
                    Saturday, Nov 21 · 10:00 AM to 12:00 PM CST
                </p>
                <p class="flex items-start gap-2 mt-1">
                    <svg
                        class="w-5 h-5 mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-gray-400"
                        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                        fill="currentColor"
                        aria-hidden="true"
                    >
                        <path
                            fill-rule="evenodd"
                            d="M12 2c-3.866 0-7 3.07-7 6.857 0 4.34 4.686 9.86 6.412 11.74a.8.8 0 0 0 1.176 0C14.314 18.717 19 13.197 19 8.857 19 5.07 15.866 2 12 2Zm0 9.5a2.5 2.5 0 1 1 0-5 2.5 2.5 0 0 1 0 5Z"
                            clip-rule="evenodd"></path>
                    </svg>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The Coffee Shop NE</span
                        ><br />2852 Johnson St NE · Minneapolis, MN</span
                    >
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                This time, the attendees are the dataset.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                We'll replicate the classic experiments of visual perception —
                build them as tiny web apps, test them on each other, and
                chart the room's own error rates against the published
                results. Coders and non-coders both essential: every
                experiment needs subjects.
            </p>

            <div class="flex justify-center mt-12 mb-8">
                <div class="max-w-xl w-full">
                    <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden bg-white">
                        <img
                            src="/images/zollner-illusion-1860.svg"
                            alt="The Zöllner illusion: long parallel lines crossed with short hatching that makes them appear to tilt"
                            class="w-full h-auto object-contain"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mt-2">
                        The Zöllner illusion (1860): every long line in this
                        figure is perfectly parallel.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                What this has to do with charts
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                Perception experiments are the empirical foundation of data
                visualization. When Cleveland and McGill ran studies like
                these on human subjects in 1984, the error rates they
                measured became the field's ranking of visual encodings:
                position beats length, length beats angle, angle beats area.
                Every time you reach for a bar chart instead of a pie chart,
                you are citing how badly their subjects judged angles. We'll
                re-measure that hierarchy on ourselves and see whether the
                room agrees with the literature.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                The illusions earn their place too. A judgment that bends
                under crosshatching, like the Zöllner lines above, is the
                same judgment you trust when you read a slope across
                gridlines or a small gap between two tall bars. Knowing where
                the eye fails, and by how much, is how you design charts the
                eye can't be fooled by.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Full details soon — RSVP on <a
                    href="https://www.meetup.com/tcplot/"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Meetup</a
                >.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                Hope to see you there!
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>
